  "narrative": {
    "northStar": "The Light Changes, the Room Stays",
    "overview": "One structure, two lights. Every surface is built once and lit twice: `data-light=\"day\"` gives limestone render, near-black ink and Broadwater teal; `data-light=\"night\"` dims the same chapters to sign-black with lamp-ivory ink. The light is chosen before first paint from Paradise Point's real time (Queensland, UTC+10, sunset by month) and a Day/Night switch in the top bar overrides it for the session. The change is a slow 1.6s crossfade, never a cut.\n\nSpace is generous and the photographs carry the page. Sections are separated by fields of colour and tone steps, never by lines or boxes. Type is a single sans family, light at display size and tightly tracked, set large enough to read solidly on the pale ground. Gold appears only where the visitor can act. The page reads top to bottom as day turning into night: limestone chapters, a teal week, then black bar, booking, address and footer.\n\nThe system rejects the category's dark-luxury template (black, gold, italic serif) used wholesale, and the cream-and-serif restaurant default. Black and gold exist here because they are the venue's own sign, and they arrive only after dark.",
    "keyCharacteristics": [
      "Two light states on one DOM; tokens swap, layout does not.",
      "One family (Work Sans 300/400/500), five fluid steps.",
      "Separation by fields and tone, never by rules, borders or shadows.",
      "Gold is the action colour and nothing else.",
      "Square, uncropped-subject photography; pill and round controls.",
      "Slow light crossfade as the signature; quiet staggered entrances."
    ],
    "rules": [
      {
        "name": "The One Action Rule",
        "body": "Gold fills only things you can press or follow (book, go, the focus ring) and the TL monogram that is its source. It never colours a heading, a field or an ornament.",
        "section": "colors"
      },
      {
        "name": "The Two Lights Rule",
        "body": "Any new surface defines its ground and ink through `--ground`, `--ground-ink` and `--ground-muted` (or sits in a fixed night field), so the Day/Night switch reaches it. A day chapter that does not dim at night is a bug.",
        "section": "colors"
      },
      {
        "name": "The Tone Step Rule",
        "body": "Adjacent sections separate by a change of field (limestone to limestone-2, black to black-2 to black-3, sea to sea-2), never by a line.",
        "section": "colors"
      },
      {
        "name": "The Solid Light Rule",
        "body": "Weight 300 is the lightest allowed, and only at lead size and above. Never set a thinner weight or a display serif on the limestone ground.",
        "section": "typography"
      },
      {
        "name": "The Name Leads Rule",
        "body": "The display step belongs to the venue name. Section titles stop at the head step.",
        "section": "typography"
      },
      {
        "name": "The Flat Field Rule",
        "body": "Surfaces never lift. If something needs emphasis, change its field (as the today row does) or its weight, not its elevation.",
        "section": "elevation"
      },
      {
        "name": "The Scrim-Only Gradient Rule",
        "body": "A gradient is allowed only as a neutral scrim between a moving photograph and type set on it. No colour gradients, no decorative washes.",
        "section": "elevation"
      }
    ],
    "dos": [
      "**Do** route every new surface's ground and ink through the light tokens so night reaches it.",
      "**Do** use the gold pill for booking and the gold round button for \"go\"; keep gold to actions.",
      "**Do** separate sections with a field or tone step (limestone to limestone-2, black to black-2 to black-3).",
      "**Do** set titles in Work Sans 300 at the head step with -0.03em tracking, and keep running text to about 30 to 34ch.",
      "**Do** show photographs square-cornered and whole, at their own aspect ratio.",
